    researchContext: "Cognitive research places the 120-125 range at a meaningful inflection point for academic performance. Meta-analyses consistently show that above approximately IQ 120 the relationship between intelligence and academic GPA strengthens, with conscientiousness becoming relatively less predictive and raw cognitive ability more so. Studies of professional licensing exam pass rates show very high first-attempt success across medicine, law, and engineering. Research by Gottfredson identifies this range as associated with the ability to independently learn from complex written materials without instruction. Occupational research shows consistent clustering at this level among attorneys, physicians, engineers, and professors.",
    realWorldScenarios: "When assigned to a new project in an unfamiliar technical domain you read primary literature directly rather than summaries, quickly identifying authoritative sources. During a legal dispute you review the contract yourself, identify the ambiguous clause, and articulate the argument more precisely than the attorney initially drafted it. While learning a new programming language you are writing functional idiomatic code within days. In a management role you identify that a recurring operational problem is a symptom of an upstream process failure, redesign the upstream process, and eliminate the downstream problem entirely — a root-cause identification that peers managed only symptom by symptom.",
